[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main()` is the whole reachable surface:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m int x = 0;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m scanf("%d", &x);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m if (x) good(); else bad();
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m return 0;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Both `good()` and `bad()` copy a zero-initialised `int source[10]` into a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m buffer and print element 0, so **stdout is `"0\n"` for every input**, stderr is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m always empty, and the exit status is always 0 (absent a signal). The `scanf`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m result is discarded and `x` is pre-initialised to 0, so every parse failure
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m lands on the `bad()` branch.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This makes stdout a weak oracle: it cannot distinguish the two branches. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mismatches below were therefore found on the exit-status and semantics axes,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m not the stdout axis.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 1 — exit status on a broken stdout pipe (real, fixed)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** With stdout connected to a pipe that has no reader:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | | termination |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C | killed by signal 13 (`SIGPIPE`) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust (before fix) | exited 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust runtime installs `SIG_IGN` for `SIGPIPE` before `main`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m runs, so a failing write returns `EPIPE` instead of raising the signal. The C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program inherits the default disposition and is killed. Compounding it, the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m translation discarded write errors (`let _ = write!(...)`), so even the `EPIPE`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m was invisible and the process fell through to `return 0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `restore_default_sigpipe()` in `translation/src/main.rs` resets
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_DFL` as the first statement of `main`, via a direct `extern
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m "C" { fn signal(...) }` declaration so the crate stays dependency-free. Both
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m programs are now killed by signal 13.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Regression test.** `broken_stdout_pipe_terminates_identically` — borrows a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m pipe from a helper `cat`, kills the helper so the read end is gone, then hands
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the write end to each program as stdout and compares how each terminated. Note
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m that a stdout-only comparison can never catch this class of bug, which is why
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the harness asserts on all three of stdout, stderr and status.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 2 — `scanf` return value for sign-then-EOF (latent, fixed)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** For input `"-"` or `"+"` (a sign immediately followed by EOF), the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m translation's `scanf_i32` returned `-1` (`EOF`, i.e. an *input* failure). glibc
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m returns `0` — a *matching* failure — because characters were already consumed,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m so it is not an input failure per the C standard.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Verified against glibc with a standalone probe (`ret` is the `scanf` return,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `x` pre-set to `-12345` to expose whether it is written):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m [-]   -> ret=0 x=-12345
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m [+]   -> ret=0 x=-12345
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m [abc] -> ret=0 x=-12345
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m []    -> ret=-1 x=-12345      <- genuine input failure
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Not observable in this program**, since `main` discards the `scanf` result
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and `x` is left untouched either way, so both values route to `bad()`. Fixed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m anyway, because a wrong-by-luck translation is a trap for the next change.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** The sign-then-EOF arm of `scanf_i32` now returns `0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Verified-correct behaviors (checked, no change needed)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Confirmed against glibc with the same probe; the Rust `scanf_i32` reproduces
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m each stored value exactly.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Whitespace skipping crosses newlines.** `%d` skips space, tab, newline,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   vertical tab, form feed and carriage return before converting — unlike
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `fgets`. `"\n\n\n7"` parses as 7 and reaches `good()`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Tests: `scanf_skips_leading_whitespace_across_newlines`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Overflow truncates a saturated `long`.** glibc accumulates `%d` into a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `long`, saturates at `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` on range error, then stores the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   low 32 bits. This produces distinctly un-obvious values, all matched:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | input | stored `int` | branch |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   |---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | `2147483648` | `-2147483648` | `good()` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | `4294967296` | `0` | **`bad()`** |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | `4294967297` | `1` | `good()` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | `-2147483649` | `2147483647` | `good()` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | `99999999999999999999999` | `-1` | `good()` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | `-99999999999999999999999` | `0` | **`bad()`** |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | `9223372036854775808` | `-1` | `good()` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | `-9223372036854775808` | `0` | **`bad()`** |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   The `4294967296` and `-99999999999999999999999` rows flip the branch relative
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   to a naive saturate-to-`INT_MAX` reading, so they are asserted rather than
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   assumed. Test: `single_item_and_maximum_values`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Conversion stops at the first non-digit.** `0x10` yields 0 (stops at `x`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   reaching `bad()`), `3.7` yields 3, `1abc` yields 1.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Test: `scanf_stops_at_first_non_digit`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Matching failure leaves `x` at its initial 0.** `abc`, `.`, `-`, `+`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `--5`, `- 5` all reach `bad()`. Test:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `scanf_matching_failures_leave_x_at_zero`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Only one conversion is consumed.** `main` calls `scanf` once; trailing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   input is never read, and the unread remainder does not alter output.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Test: `only_the_first_conversion_is_consumed`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Empty and whitespace-only input.** Both give `scanf` EOF, leaving `x == 0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and reaching `bad()`. Tests: `empty_input_takes_the_bad_branch`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `whitespace_only_input_is_eof_for_scanf`, `stdin_closed_immediately`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **stdout on a closed descriptor (`>&-`).** Writes fail with `EBADF`, which
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   raises no signal; both programs ignore the error and exit 0. Distinct from
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Mismatch 1. Test: `stdout_redirected_to_a_closed_descriptor`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`argv` is ignored.** `main()` takes no parameters.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Test: `command_line_arguments_are_ignored`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Non-UTF-8, NUL and high bytes on stdin** are handled identically; the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   harness pipes raw `&[u8]`, never `String`. Test: `binary_and_nul_bytes`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Inputs far larger than any buffer** (100,000-byte digit, zero, whitespace
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and alpha runs). Test: `very_long_inputs`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Deliberate non-reproduction: the `alloca` overflow in `bad()`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m data = (int *)alloca(10);          /* 10 bytes */
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m for (i = 0; i < 10; i++) data[i] = source[i];   /* writes 40 bytes */
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `bad()` allocates 10 bytes and writes 40 — a stack buffer overflow, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m undefined behavior in C (this is the CWE demonstration the file exists for).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `good()` is the corrected form, `alloca(10 * sizeof(int))`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The translation uses a correctly sized buffer in both functions and does **not**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m recreate the out-of-bounds write. Rationale: the only thing the overflow exposes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is `data[0]`, which is 0 in both functions because `source` is zero-initialised.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m UB has no defined behavior to match, so there is nothing to be faithful *to* —
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the choice is between reproducing one incidental observation of it or matching
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the observable contract. Observationally the C program prints `0\n` here, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `repeated_runs_are_deterministic` confirms this over 25 runs of each branch,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m so the C side is stable and the two agree.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The one respect in which the C and Rust `bad()` genuinely differ is memory
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m safety, and that is not observable through stdout, stderr or exit status. If the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C were rebuilt with a hardening flag that turns the overflow into a trap
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`-fsanitize=address`, or a stack protector that this overflow happens to trip),
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the C would abort and the Rust would not. The suite builds the C program with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the unmodified `c_src/CMakeLists.txt`, which sets no such flags, so this does
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m not arise as configured — but it is the known limit of the equivalence claim
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m rather than something the tests establish.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Unreachable code
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `printLine(const char *)`, including its `line != NULL` branch, is never called
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m from `main`. No input can reach it, so no test covers it; it is retained in the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust as `print_line` under `#[allow(dead_code)]` for structural fidelity with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the original translation unit.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Status
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
